 underestimated by most people  in this video we're going to show you  exactly why it is so important  to consistently negotiate your salary  first of all  it really matters for your lifetime  earnings  it makes a huge difference and research  has shown that people  who consistently negotiate their salary  over their entire lifetime  earn about 1 million dollars more  throughout their entire career  let's start with getting more i want to  show you a simple theoretical graph  that illustrates how salary negotiations  really impact your salary and how it  increases your earnings over your entire  lifetime in this case example we have  two fictional people  christian who does not negotiate his  salary so for him i assumed a one  percent increase  basically in line with inflation over  each year on the other hand there's  theodore  he negotiates his salary every three  years  and he achieves a five percent average  negotiation success  which is really in line with what you  can expect by the end of his career  theodore will earn about 140 000 each  year  well christian will be stuck at roughly  eighty thousand  so this is already a huge difference if  you add up all the years where theodore  earns  more and more money than christian  throughout that  entire career the difference is enormous  in this case example it's about 1.2  million dollar  i've oversimplified this obviously but  it really proves the point  how important it is to not be afraid of  negotiating a salary  usually your employer will respect you  more if you ask  what you are worth  so the one thing is to freshly negotiate  your salary once you get a new job  but the other thing is once you're  already in the job then also to be able  to negotiate  regular raises and this is the topic  that we are going to talk about in this  section  in this first lecture i want to talk  about what is realistic  what can you ask for a race because this  is indeed the question that at least i  get most frequently when i discuss this  topic with friends and colleagues  and here it often helps really argue  with some data so in this  lecture i want to help you by providing  a little orientation some  points of data that might help you to  guide this discussion and the first  thing to be aware of is of course the  inflation rate measured by the consumer  price index  this measures how much prices increase  on average for the consumer in every  single year  of course these values differ  significantly come to my country  so you should take this in consideration  depending on your geography that you  work in  so pulling some data for germany you can  see here that the values pretty much  fluctuate between one and two percent  per year and this is exactly the average  that you can expect for most developed  countries so one to two percent per year  annual consumer price inflation so make  sure to research this value for your  country  the index might be called a bit  differently there might be different  indices  measuring concepts like this as well for  your country so look it up in a second  similar index that might be helpful here  is the labor cost  index that shows you how much on average  the price for labor so the salaries  in the country have gone up over time  and often these values are more or less  similar in some countries the one might  be higher than the other  but here again if on average salaries go  up by two percent every year  this is where can give you a certain  indication of what you should target  though of course you should not really  be happy with just  getting a salary on the level of  inflation because if you exactly meet  the inflation number  you do not really have a salary increase  just the fact that now you can buy  next year as much things with your money  as  last year or to put it differently if  you're not even managed to get a salary  raise that needs inflation  you will lose purchasing power you  pretty much lose value all for your  money year on year  and for these reasons compensation for  inflation should pretty much be the  minimum  the minimum amount that you ask for and  that you get  when you go into these conversations so  this is the one end of the spectrum then  what is the other hand of the spectrum  and just to give you a ballpark figure  here one number that you can expect to  receive  if you work in a so-called accelerated  career environment  such as consulting or banking is roughly  an  average of a salary increase of 10 per  year so i'd argue that there are only  very very few careers very very few  roads where you can get a long-term  average salary increase of more than 10  per year so aim for these 10  in these very high pace accelerated  korean environments  but of course there's a reason that  these people get this type of money of  course in these  jobs often need to work a lot the  performance pressure on you is quite  significant  so if you are in a more general standard  nine to five  corporate job probably this is a bit  high and should be then adjusted to a  bit of a lower number  so one to two percent this is the  minimum that you should get  this is also actually what some  companies provide just by themselves as  a yearly inflation compensation to the  employees and then on top of that you  should ask for an increase that is  up to 10 based on the type of career  that you're in  and of course here the importance is  also to see this as a long-term average  so it's absolutely okay to maybe one  year not get that much of a raise  if then after two years after three  years you get an  even higher salary increase maybe if you  step up to the next higher position but  make sure that on average  these are the targets that you can  achieve  welcome to this section in which we will  talk about how to increase the overall  value  for both sides on the table this concept  is called  value creation and negotiations and it's  a very important concept  that you can also apply to salary  negotiations  so far we've really simplified the  salary negotiation  and only talked about your total  compensation  as one dollar figure but actually  there are many many different elements  that make up your total compensation  and you can make clever use of these  different elements  to create value in the negotiation it is  really a common trap or mistake people  make  in salary negotiations because they  simplify  the many different elements into one  dollar figure  and this way they go heads on with their  employer instead of thinking about  clever and nuanced ways to create value  the key insight here is the following  you should aim to negotiate for salary  components that are worth more  to you and worth less to your employer  by this way you can basically create  value  this will enable you to grow your total  compensation  by a lot your employer might be bound by  certain salary bands that are  very specific to your role and maybe you  just barely miss the qualification for a  more senior role that would also have  a different salary range of course in  this case you can try to negotiate for a  much faster review of your current role  and your promotion this gives you a much  faster runway to the next promotion  and therefore a much faster access to a  higher salary another example would be  maybe you want a different job title  because you know that if you switch  jobs it will give you a much better  salary range  this will actually cost your current  employer nothing if they can offend it  against other employees of course  but it could be worth a lot to you to  give you some ideas of  salary components that you can negotiate  for and that other people have  negotiated for  i will now show you a brief overview  besides the overall component of course  your  annual cash compensation you can  negotiate for  vacation days you can negotiate for a  sign on bonus in case you're  switching to a new employer other common  types of compensation components can be  your relocation coverage it could be a  tuition allowance  your job title as we discussed in the  previous example  it could be the early review it could be  a company car or maybe a mobility budget  or  in case of particular smaller companies  or startups  maybe you can negotiate for a visa  package or an equity option package that  gives you  a really strong incentive in the long  run to stay with the company which is  also of course interesting to your  employer  you can really extend this list to many  other elements i've shown some here  on this slide maybe if you already are a  father or a mother  you can negotiate for child care support  a parental leave  or maybe for other people a wellness or  fitness program  just look through all the components i'm  showing you here on this slide  and get creative you will find that by  opening new opportunities for your  employer to  compensate you in some sort of way be it  monetary  or in case of job titles giving you  future possibilities and future options  you can really create a lot of value for  yourself in the negotiation  as you're preparing for your next  negotiation write down the different  salary components that would be relevant  for your negotiation  now we hope that you got value out of  this video and if it is the case please  hit the like button for the youtube  algorithm and also subscribe to my  channel to stay up to date on all my  content if you want to continue your  learning journey with firm learning  i will link above here a video on  problem solving really one of the key  consulting skills  check it out to continue learning and if  you like the topic of salary negotiation  please again check out the link to the  full course  that we created on this topic in the  video description  if you want to see more from me also  follow me on my instagram my handle is  from learning or  sign up to the mailing list link in the  video description and i also want to say  thank you to all the members of firm  learning your support is very much  appreciated and check out the join  button if you are interested to become a  members way this is highly from from  learning i'm releasing new videos  every single saturday so stay tuned for  next saturday  happy easter to all of you cheers and  bye bye

